{"id": "section-body-24379", "title": "Non-melanoma Skin Cancers", "chapterId": "chapter-twenty-two", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "MALIGNANT DISEASE CONDITIONS", "Cancer of The Skin", "Non-melanoma Skin Cancers"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-24379", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Non-melanoma Skin Cancers", "runs": [{"text": "Non-melanoma"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Skin"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Cancers"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "534"}}, {"id": "body-24380", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) and squamous cell (SCC) are the most common non melanoma skin cancers. SCC is more aggressive than BCC and has the potential to metastasis. The main cause of these skin cancers is overexposure to Ultraviolet radiation. Risk factors include light-colored skin (e.g. albinism), previous burn and immunosuppression e.g. after transplant or HIV infection", "runs": [{"text": "Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) and squamous cell (SCC) are the most common non melanoma skin cancers. SCC is more aggressive"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "than"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "BCC"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "has"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "potential"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "metastasis."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "The"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "main"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cause"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "these"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "skin"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cancers"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "overexposure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Ultraviolet radiation. Risk factors include light-colored skin (e.g. albinism), previous burn and immunosuppression e.g. after transplant or HIV infection"}]}, {"id": "body-24382", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Prevention or early detection; is through frequent self–health check–up or screening exercise and prompt treatment of early skin lesions. For light skinned people–avoid UV light especially people with Albinism.", "runs": [{"text": "Prevention", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "or", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "early", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "detection;", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "through"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "frequent"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "self–health"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "check–up"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "screening"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "exercise"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "prompt"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "treatment"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "early skin lesions. For light skinned people–avoid UV light especially people with Albinism."}]}, {"id": "body-24384", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Clinical Presentation", "runs": [{"text": "Clinical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Presentation"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-24385", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "The most common warning sign of skin cancer is a change in the appearance on exposed areas of the skin, such as a new growth or a sore that will not heal. Occasionally, such changes may appear on an old burn area.", "runs": [{"text": "The most common warning sign of skin cancer is a change in the appearance on exposed areas of the skin, such as a new growth or a sore that will not heal. Occasionally, such changes may appear on an old burn area."}]}, {"id": "body-24388", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Investigations", "runs": [{"text": "Investigations"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-24389", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "None if the lesion is small", "runs": [{"text": "None"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "if"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "lesion"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " small"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "452"}}, {"id": "body-24390", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Local X-ray if bone involvement is suspected", "runs": [{"text": "Local"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "X-ray"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "if"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bone"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "involvement"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "suspected"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "452"}}, {"id": "body-24391", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "CXR", "runs": [{"text": "CXR"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "452"}}, {"id": "body-24394", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Management", "runs": [{"text": "Management"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-24396", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Biopsy – preferably excisional biopsy, where possible, for histology", "runs": [{"text": "Biopsy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "–"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "preferably"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "excisional"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "biopsy,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "where possible, for histology"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "451"}}, {"id": "body-24397", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "CT or MRI is needed for suspected nodal and bone involvement.", "runs": [{"text": "CT"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "MRI"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "needed"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "suspected"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "nodal"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bone "}, {"text": "involvement."}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "451"}}, {"id": "body-24398", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "PET/CT when indicated", "runs": [{"text": "PET/CT"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "when"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "indicated"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "451"}}, {"id": "body-24400", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Surgery is the primary treatment. Wide local excision that achieves negative surgical margins is adequate. For Node positive BCC or SCC Resection with Lymph node dissection. Skin grafting may be required after surgery. Amputation is sometimes done for palliation. Locally destructive methods such as curetting or cryotherapy may be employed.", "runs": [{"text": "Surgery is the primary treatment. Wide local excision that achieves negative surgical margins is adequate. For Node positive BCC or SCC Resection with Lymph node dissection. Skin grafting may be required after surgery. Amputation is sometimes done for palliation. Locally destructive methods such as curetting or cryotherapy may be employed."}]}, {"id": "body-24402", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Radiotherapy", "runs": [{"text": "Radiotherapy"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-24403", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Indication: Positive margin, high-grade disease, or inoperable tumour.", "runs": [{"text": "Indication:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Positive"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "margin,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "high-grade"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "disease,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "inoperable"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "tumour."}]}, {"id": "body-24404", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Dose: 60-66/ in 30-33 Fractions EBRT for Radical Treatment, 30Gy in 10 Fractions for palliative treatment", "runs": [{"text": "Dose:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "60-66/"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "30-33"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Fractions"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "EBRT"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Radical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Treatment,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "30Gy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "10"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Fractions"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "palliative"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "treatment"}]}, {"id": "body-24406", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Pharmacological Management", "runs": [{"text": "Pharmacological"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Management"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-24407", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "S: 5-fluorouracil topical cream, apply every 12 -24 hours, for very superficial lesions or carcinoma in situ", "runs": [{"text": "S:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "5-fluorouracil"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "topical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cream,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "apply"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "every"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "12"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "-24"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "hours,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "very"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "superficial"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "lesions"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "carcinoma"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "situ"}]}, {"id": "body-24409", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Systemic chemotherapy is given during radiotherapy as a radiosensitizer.", "runs": [{"text": "Systemic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "chemotherapy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "given"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "during"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "radiotherapy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "radiosensitizer."}]}, {"id": "body-24410", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "S: cisplatin (IV) 40 mg/m2 to a maximum of 70 mg is given weekly during radiation therapy.", "runs": [{"text": "S:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "cisplatin"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(IV)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "40"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "mg/m2"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " 